3.16.4.m:
The kindling [quarter] of the quarters, lying near, winning the sun, may Mādhava protect our sweet semen.
May the god Agni, hard to overcome, unbeguiled, protect this dominion, may he protect us.
May the Rathantara protect us through the Sāmans, the Gāyatrī of all forms among the metres;
May the Trivṛt stoma with its division, the ocean, the wind protect this vigor for us through the day.
The fierce one of the quarters, overpowering, bestowing strength, pure on the bright day, vigorous;
Do you protect us from this by Indra's overlordships, uphold this great dominion on all sides.
The Bṛhat Sāman upholding dominion, whose bull has grown, the Triṣṭubh, vigor adorned, fierce with heroes;
May Indra protect this middle with the fifteenfold stoma, with the wind, with the ocean.
The eastern of the quarters, accompanied by glory, glorious; the All-Gods, the rainy season among days, sun-winning;
May I be able to achieve that in the Vairūpa Sāman; I cause him to enter among the clans with the Jagatī.
May this dominion be hard to overcome, vigor unassailable, forceful, full of force;
The All-Gods with the seventeenfold [stoma], brilliance, this dominion, fierce with the watery wind.
The supporter of the quarters upholds this dominion; may the vigor of the reaches of the quarters be friendly;
Mitra and Varuṇa, note [us] with the autumn, with the day; grant great shelter to our realm.
In the Vairāja Sāman is my thought, with the Anuṣṭubh is manly vigor [and] force gathered;
This dominion is friendly, bestowing moisture; O Mitra and Varuṇa, protect [it] with your overlordships.
The sovereign of the quarters, having a thousand Sāmans, full of force; may the winter season protect us with its division;
With wind seeking help, lofty like the Śakvarī; may the [quarter] dripping with ghee protect us [...];
Sun-winning, yielding well, rich in milk for us; may she who drips with ghee favor this sacrifice;
You are the guardian, walking in front and behind; O Bṛhaspati, yoke the speech relating to Yama.
The upward one of the quarters, the delighting ones reaching among plants; Savitṛ [favor] us with the year, with the day;
The Revat Sāman, and the Atichandas among metres; may she without enemies be gentle to us.
The stoma in the thirty-threefold, the mistress of the world; with wind shining bright, sing praise to us;
Dripping with ghee under Savitṛ's overlordship, rich in milk; may the bestowal, the quarter, be for us.
May Anumati today approve our sacrifice among the gods;
And may Agni, carrier of oblations, be a joy to the worshipper.
Do you, O Anumati, approve, and make well-being for us;
Impel us to purpose, to skill; may he prolong our lifespans.
May Vaiśvānara come to our aid from afar,
Agni with the draft of praise.
Asked in heaven...
The firm one of the quarters, wife of Viṣṇu, not terrible, ruling over this [dominion], who is the Manotā of force;
May Bṛhaspati, Mātariśvan, and Vāyu, the winds blowing together, sing praise to us.
The prop of heaven, support of the earth, ruling over this moving world, the wife of Viṣṇu,
Spacious, invigorating, prosperous, may she be auspicious to us in the lap of Aditi.
With what help for us...
Who yokes today to the pole the draft-oxen of Ṛta, laborious, radiant, bearing grudge against wrong,
Having arrows in their mouth, piercing the heart, bringing joy? Whoever accomplishes their maintenance, he shall live.

4.1.2.m:
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
Prajāpati indeed knows the plants joint by joint.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he cuts the barhis with a horse-rib sickle,
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
"You are cow-dwelling," [he says];
he places wealth in the sacrificer.
"Scorched is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"Forward has this Dhiṣaṇā gone to the barhis," [he says];
Dhiṣaṇā is indeed knowledge;
with knowledge indeed he goes to it.
"Fashioned by Manu, fashioned according to self-nature," [he says];
for it was fashioned by Manu, fashioned according to self-nature.
"With it the sages carry [the sacrifice] forward from the east," [he says];
the Brahmins are the sages;
the ṛṣis are the sages.
"Pleasing to the gods, here to sit upon the barhis," [he says];
he makes it pleasing to the gods.
"You are impelled by the gods," [he says];
whatever [exists] here, that is impelled by the gods.
As one performs an action having announced it to a superior, even so, having announced it to the gods, he cuts the barhis.
As much as he marks out, all that he cuts.
If he does not cut all of it, he makes that redundant in the sacrifice.
What becomes redundant in the sacrifice, with that he strengthens his rival.
He marks out a single clump;
all of that he cuts,
for the non-redundancy of the sacrifice.
"The crest of Viṣṇu," [he says];
Viṣṇu's crest was indeed cut off;
it entered the earth.
If he should not cast off the first [stalk], he would meet with misfortune.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I cut you, the barhis, the seat of the gods," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he cuts it with the deities.
"May I prosper below your joint, not above," [he says];
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
"May I, your cutter, not be injured," [he says];
as much as an unknowing Adhvaryu thus cuts the barhis, so much of his own self is diminished;
the self of him who knows thus is not diminished.
"From here, O barhis, grow out with a hundred shoots!" [he says];
he places abundance in the plants.
"May we grow out with a thousand shoots!" [he says];
he utters a blessing.
"You are the girdle of Aditi," [he says];
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
he makes it her girdle.
"The tying-cord of Indrāṇī," [he says];
Indrāṇī indeed in the beginning, desiring prosperity, tied herself up among the deities;
she prospered;
[it is] for prosperity;
he prospers who knows thus.
He ties together the barhis;
the barhis is indeed offspring;
[it is] for the continuity of offspring, for the non-scattering of offspring.
Therefore offspring are tied together by sinews.
"May Pūṣan tie your knot," [he says];
Pūṣan is indeed nourishment;
he places nourishment in the sacrificer.
"May it not stand against you," [he says];
[it is] for the non-injury of the sacrificer.
"The waters, the Aśvins, the seven ṛṣis have cleansed you; / With the rays of the sun I take the barhis, the banner of the dawns," [he says];
the Aśvins are indeed the physicians of the gods;
the two heal it.
"With the arms of Indra I raise you up," [he says];
with the arms of Indra he raises it up.
"With the head of Bṛhaspati I bring [you]," [he says];
Bṛhaspati is indeed the sacred utterance;
with the sacred utterance he brings it.
"The broad atmosphere," [he says];
[it is] for fulfillment.
"I seat you on the back of Aditi," [he says];
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
on her he has seated it.
"You are the barhis that goes to the gods," with this [formula] he addresses it once seated;
indeed, much of this, when it was being brought the day before, spills here and there;
he has delivered it unspilled to the deities;
[it is] for the non-injury of the sacrificer.

4.1.3.m:
On the previous day they prepare the kindling and the barhis.
Taking hold of the sacrifice, he fasts.
Prajāpati created creatures.
His two pots slipped down.
With these two pots he restored [them].
In that these two pots are [used],
he restores the two pots of Prajāpati.
"Be purified for the divine work," [he says];
he purifies them for the gods.
"You are the filter of the Vasus," [he says];
this is indeed the portion of the Vasus;
he makes it for them.
He covers [it] with the filter;
he unites the milk of plants and of cattle.
He restrains his speech,
for the support of the sacrifice.
He sits holding [it];
for, as it were, they milk while holding.
"You are sky; you are earth," [he says];
with these two he heats it.
"The cauldron of Mātariśvan," [he says];
free atmosphere indeed is the cauldron of Mātariśvan,
for the support of the atmosphere.
"Which one have you milked?
She is all-life;
she is all-being;
she is all-action," [he says].
Yonder [sky] indeed is all-life;
the atmosphere is all-being;
this [earth] is all-action.
He causes these worlds to yield in proper order;
he who knows thus milks these worlds yielded in proper order.
He addresses three [cows] with a yajus;
three indeed are these worlds;
he places sap in these worlds;
therefore creatures live upon these worlds.
"Offered is the drop; offered is the spark," he addresses [it],
for non-spilling.
"For Indra, for the gods, milk much oblation!" he proclaims three times;
for the gods are thrice-true.
He should not milk into a wooden vessel;
the wooden vessel is fiery;
he would sacrifice with an exhausted oblation.
Regarding that the Dātreyas used to say:
"The oblation has the sacrificial cake for its mouth;
not from this side is there an exhaustion of the oblation of the cake;
one should milk indeed."
A Śūdra should not milk;
he is born from non-being;
he would become non-being.
What passes through the filter is oblation;
a Śūdra should not milk the agnihotra,
for that they do not purify.
"Mix together, O righteous ones, sweetest with the wave, / mingling milk with milk, gladdening, for the winning of wealth!"
Thus he pours [it] in return,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
"I curdle you as a portion for Indra with Soma," [he says];
he makes it Soma.
Continuous indeed is the Soma-drink for him who, knowing thus, drinks the sānnāyya.
"You are unexhausted; for Viṣṇu you [I take]," [he says];
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he makes it unexhausted for the sacrifice.
"O Viṣṇu, protect the oblation," [he says];
as one says, "This is your share, protect it," so indeed he says to Viṣṇu: "This is your oblation, protect it."
He should not cover it with an earthen vessel;
the oblation would belong to the Fathers as deity.
He should cover it with a wooden vessel;
the wooden vessel is fiery;
Agni himself protects the oblation.
It should be made watery;
water is demon-slaying;
demons do not cross water;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.

4.1.4.m:
He who sacrifices without taking hold of faith—gods and men do not put faith in his sacrifice.
He leads forward the waters;
the waters are faith;
taking hold of faith he sacrifices.
The waters are the sacrifice;
having extended the sacrifice, he performs [it].
The waters are the dear domain of the gods;
having brought forward the dear domain of the gods, he performs [it].
The waters are truth;
taking hold of truth, he sacrifices.
The waters are demon-slaying;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
The waters are a thunderbolt;
he hurls a thunderbolt at his rival,
for laying [him] low.
The waters are faith;
gods and men put faith in the sacrifice of him, knowing thus, for whom one knowing thus brings forward the waters.
"O divine waters, moving first," [he says];
he brings forward the sacrifice.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"Wide atmosphere," [he says],
for the sake of the successful sacrifice.
"Scorched is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"You are the yoke-pin; injure the injurer," [he says];
this Agni is indeed named 'belonging to the yoke';
if he should pass over [him] without taking hold, he would afflict the sacrificer with heat.
Two indeed are a man's rivals:
he whom he hates and he who hates him; those two he afflicts with heat.

4.1.5.m:
"You are of the gods the best conveyer, the most winning, the most satisfying, the most pleasing, the best caller of the gods," [he says];
he makes it for the gods the best conveyer, the most winning, the most satisfying, the most pleasing, the best caller of the gods.
"You are the unswerving cart of oblation;
be firm,
do not waver," [he says];
he makes it firm.
"You are the stride of Viṣṇu;
broad for the wind," [he says].
Whatever the wind does not blow upon, that belongs to Varuṇa.
He makes it un-Varuṇic.
"I gaze upon you with the eye of Mitra," [he says];
he makes them friendly.
"Take strength," [he says];
he places strength in the sacrificer.
"For abundance to you," [he says];
he causes them to prosper.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan,
let the five restrain [you];
for your protection, not for malice;
I pour you out pleasing to Agni," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he pours them out with the deities.
"Let the five restrain," [he says];
the seasons indeed are five;
he thus gratifies the seasons;
gratified, they serve him.
"For your protection, not for malice," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"Pleasing to Agni, you," [he says];
he makes them pleasing to Agni.
Moreover, to whatever deity he pours out, pleasing to that one he makes them.
"This for the gods, this for us together," [he says],
for separation.
"Let the doorposts stand firm," [he says];
when the oblation was taken, these worlds trembled upwards;
the gods made them firm with this sacrificial formula;
when he knows this sacrificial formula, [it is] for the support of these worlds.
"I have been freed from the fetter of Varuṇa," [he says];
he is freed from the fetter of Varuṇa,
for the non-injury of himself.
"I have looked upon the light of heaven," [he says];
he enters as it were into darkness, into the cart's enclosure;
he looks forth upon the light of heaven.
"Light belonging to all men," [he says];
that sun indeed is the light belonging to all men.
"I follow the broad atmosphere," [he says].
If he were to say "Go [apart]!", the Adhvaryu would run away.
"I follow the broad atmosphere," [he says];
the Adhvaryu does not run away.
"In the lap of Aditi I seat you," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
upon her he has seated them.
"O Agni, protect the oblation," [he says, and] sets [it] down in front;
Agni protects the oblation.

4.1.6.m:
"Purified are you two by the mind of Viṣṇu," [he says];
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
for the sacrifice he purifies the two.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies them upwards.
"Pleasing to Agni, you," [he says];
he makes them pleasing to Agni.
Moreover, for whichever deity he sprinkles [them], pleasing to that one he makes them.
"What impure thing has touched you, purify that," [he says];
he purifies them.
"You are the skin of Aditi," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
he makes it the skin of her.
"Shaken off is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
He spreads out the black-antelope skin with its neck turned westward;
therefore cattle approach the sacrifice facing westward.
"You are a broad pressing-stone, belonging to the tree," [he says];
he makes it a pressing-stone.
"You are Agni's tongue, the releasing of speech," [he says];
for this is Agni's tongue, the releasing of speech;
for when cattle eat of the plants, then they release speech.
"For life, you two," [he says];
he places life in the sacrificer.
"You are a great pressing-stone, belonging to the tree," [he says];
he makes it a pressing-stone.
"Prepare the oblation for the gods," [he says];
he prepares it for the gods.
"Slain is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
"O maker of oblation, come!" [he says];
whoever is the maker of oblation of the gods, him he calls.
Three times he calls;
for the gods have three truths.
When Manu Śraddhādeva was sacrificing, speech that slays the asuras entered the sacrificial weapons.
As she was speaking, as many asuras as heard [her], so many ceased to be on that day.
A brāhmaṇa who knows thus, having settled in the midst of his rivals, should sacrifice;
as many of his rivals as hear the sacrificial weapons, their brilliance, sensory faculty, and manly vigor he takes away.
"You are a cock, honey-tongued; with you may we conquer encounter after encounter," [he says];
by this indeed the sacrificer conquers his rival encounter after encounter.
"Speak nourishment, speak strength, speak increase of wealth," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.

4.1.7.m:
"You are rain-grown;
let rain-grown [water] receive you," [he says],
for a firm foundation.
With blood indeed the gods appeased the demons away from cattle, with husks from plants.
In that he says, "Winnowed away is the demon, winnowed away is malice,"
with just so much he appeases the demons away from the sacrifice.
"Let god Savitṛ separate you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he separates them.
"Separate with a good sifting," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
He should make them well-husked;
he makes them pure and sacrificial.
Three times he husks [them];
for the gods have three truths.
"You are the skin of Aditi," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Aditi;
he makes it the skin of her.
"Shaken off is the demon," [he says];
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
He spreads out the black-antelope skin with its neck turned westward;
therefore cattle approach the sacrifice facing westward.
"You are the support of Aditi," [he says, and] places the yoke-pin beneath.
These two [worlds] were together;
they separated by the measure of a yoke-pin after yoke-pin.
In that he places the yoke-pin beneath,
[it is] for the upholding of these two.
"You are grain; gladden the gods," [he says];
by the vigor of this sacrificial formula, as far as one deity desires,
as far as one [desires], so far her oblation spreads out;
he spreads out with offspring and cattle who knows thus.
"For out-breath you, for in-breath you, for through-breath you," [he says];
out-breath, in-breath, and through-breath—these he places in the sacrificer.
"Touch together along the long reach of the hand;
for life, you two," [he says];
he places life in him.
"I look upon you with the eye of Mitra," [he says];
he makes them friendly.
"Let god Savitṛ, the golden-handed, receive you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he receives them.

4.1.8.m:
"Burnt up is the demon," [he says],
for striking away the demons.
When he rolls a live coal underneath,
by that he places light in this world;
when [he rolls one] above, by that [he places light] in the atmosphere.
Yonder sun becomes light for him in yonder world.
Moreover, it becomes daylight for him in yonder world; all these worlds become full of light for him who knows thus.
He puts down three [potsherds] joined together;
three indeed are these worlds;
for the upholding of these worlds.
"O Agni, cast away the raw-flesh-eating Agni," [he says];
having struck away that which is raw-flesh-eating, carrion-eating, he puts down the sacrificial [potsherds] on the place of sacrifice to the gods.
"You are firm, make firm the earth," [he says];
he makes the earth firm.
"You are the supporter, make firm the atmosphere," [he says];
he makes the atmosphere firm.
"You are the maintainer, make firm the sky," [he says];
he makes the sky firm.
"You are the ordinance, make all the regions firm," [he says];
he makes the quarters firm.
Prajāpati indeed, when he came into being, came into being in just this measure.
First one potsherd of the head comes into being,
then the second, then the third, then the fourth, then the fifth, then the sixth, then the seventh, then the eighth.
In that he puts down eight,
the sacrificer thereby prepares himself;
prepared, he goes after [him] in yonder world.
In that he puts down eight,
thereby he attains the gāyatrī meter;
in that [he puts down] eleven,
thereby the triṣṭubh;
in that [he puts down] twelve,
thereby the jagatī.
Moreover, he puts down all [potsherds] commensurate with the meters.
"You are piled up, you are piled around," [he says];
he makes them possessive of yajus formulas.
"Be heated with the heat of the gharma of the Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, Bhṛgus, and Aṅgirases," [he says];
he heats them with the heat of these deities.
"Which potsherds the wise pile upon the gharma, / may Indra and Vāyu unyoke them in accordance with Pūṣan's ordinance," [he says];
by the yajus they are yoked, by the yajus they are unyoked.

4.1.9.m:
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I pour you together," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, with the deities he pours it together.
He pours it together over a strainer;
he makes it an oblation.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies them upwards.
He adds the waters;
the waters are immortality;
he makes the oblation immortal.
"Let the waters unite with the plants, / the plants with the sap, / the wealthy ones with the jagatīs," [he says];
some of these indeed invigorate the others;
the waters invigorate the plants,
the plants [invigorate] the waters.
"Auspicious waters have united with the auspicious," [he says];
the waters are the wealthy ones,
the plants are sweet,
cattle are the jagatīs;
having united the waters, the plants, and cattle for him, he makes them together full of sweetness.
"You are the head of Makha," he says;
Makha is the sacrifice;
this head of his is prepared.
"Let the clans sit; / to give birth to you," [he says];
he makes a pair.
"You are the gharma, possessing all life; / O gharma, rest in the gharma; / spread wide; / may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide," [he says];
he spreads it out.
"Spread wide towards the earth and the sky," [he says];
he spreads these two.
This gharma indeed is placed on the fire every half-month;
unappeased, it might burn up the sacrificer's cattle with its glow.
When he carries fire around it,
he makes it cattle;
for appeasement, so as not to burn [them].
Three times he carries fire around it;
three indeed are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
The demons wished to slay the sacrificial cake when it was set upon the fire;
there was in the sky an Agni named Nāka, slayer of demons;
he struck the demons away from it.
In that he says,
"Let god Savitṛ cook you on the highest vault of the earth,"
[it is] for striking away the demons.
The head of the sacrifice is this sacrificial cake;
the veda-grass is the hair;
as are the potsherds of the head, so are the potsherds;
as is the brain, so is the sacrificial cake.
It should be made well-cooked,
for purity.
If he were not to cover the sacrificial cake, the brain would become visible.
Because he covers it,
therefore the brain is hidden; he causes it to take skin;
therefore the brain is covered around. He covers it with ashes;
therefore bone is covered with flesh. He covers it with embers;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
If he were to cover it with ashes without causing it to take skin, the Adhvaryu would become gray-haired;
he covers it with embers;
the Adhvaryu does not become gray-haired.
Those gods indeed did not find on whom they could wipe off the cruel part of the sacrifice.
Agni said:
"I will produce for you the one on whom you will wipe off the cruel part of the sacrifice."
He cast a live coal onto the waters;
from that Ekata was born;
a second [coal];
from that Dvita;
a third [coal];
from that Trita.
In that he fashioned [them] from the waters, that is the water-nature of the Āpeyas;
in that he fashioned [them] from himself, that is the self-nature of the Ātmeyas.
He offers inside the altar;
thereby he secures it.
He pours clarified butter over it,
for cooking;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
Those gods went wiping off [the guilt];
they wiped it off onto one on whom the sun rises while sleeping.
One on whom the sun rises while sleeping is the sūryābhyudita;
onto one on whom the sun sets [while sleeping, is] the sūryābhinimrukta;
onto one with black teeth, the black-toothed one;
onto one with bad nails, the bad-nailed one;
onto an agredadhū, the agredadhū;
onto an elder brother passed over in marriage, the parivitta;
onto a younger brother married before his elder, the parivividāna;
onto a slayer of a hero, the hero-slayer.
The guilt does not pass beyond the slayer of an embryo.

4.1.10.m:
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, with the deities he takes it.
"Thousand-pointed, hundred-edged," [he says];
he whets a vajra, intending to strike at his rival.
"O earth, place of sacrifice to the gods, let me not harm the root of your plants," [he says],
for the non-harming of the plants.
"Go to the enclosure, the cow-pen," [he says];
the enclosure, the cow-pen, is the metres;
he makes the metres an enclosure, a cow-pen for him.
"Let Parjanya rain for you," [he says];
from Parjanya he wins rain.
"Bind, O god Savitṛ, with a hundred fetters in the farthest distance," [he says];
two indeed are a man's rivals: whom he hates and who hates him;
those he binds in the farthest distance.
"Whoever hates us and whom we hate, him bind here; / let him not be freed from here," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Let not your drop fall to heaven," [he says];
what is her sap, that is the drop;
upon that these creatures live;
that he holds in her,
so that it does not fall.
"Let not your Araru fly up to heaven," [he says];
Araru was indeed an Asura by name;
he lay crouching against the earth;
Indra perceived him;
he struck him away: "Away [with] Araru, the non-sacrificer to the gods, from the earth!";
he wished to fly to heaven;
he repelled him from heaven: "Let not your Araru fly up to heaven."
He who knowing thus carries the stambayajus, from these worlds the sacrificer excludes his rival.
Three times he carries it;
three indeed are these worlds;
from these worlds he excludes his rival.
Silently he carries it a fourth time;
from whichever world is unexpressed, boundless, from that he excludes his rival.
This earth belonged to the Asuras;
the gods said:
"Give us [a share] of this earth."
[The Asuras said:] "Speak yourselves!"
Then the Vasus won the eastern quarter,
the Rudras the southern,
the Ādityas the western,
the Maruts the northern.
Then the gods found this [earth] from the Asuras;
then the gods excluded the Asuras from these worlds.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
When thus with these deities he encompasses the vedi,
this [earth] the sacrificer appropriates from his rival;
from this he excludes his rival;
he prospers himself, his rival is defeated.
From the earth the sacrificial and the non-sacrificial departed in different directions;
the sacrificial departed towards the east and north,
the non-sacrificial towards the south and west.
He should make the vedi sloping towards the east and north;
he makes them pure and sacrificial.
Moreover, whatever cruel deed they did to her while digging out, that he made non-cruel;
he appeases it;
and also for the separation of the sacrificial and the non-sacrificial.
He extends his arms eastward;
therewith he encompasses the Āhavanīya;
westward [are] the buttocks [of the vedi];
he encompasses the Gārhapatya.
The demons drink along the root;
if he were to cut it with his fingernail, he would become bad-nailed;
he cuts it with the wooden sword;
the wooden sword is a vajra;
with the vajra he slays the demons.

4.1.11.m:
Whoever, not knowing the yoking of the ladles, responds when addressed with "O Adhvaryu!", is cut off from the deities, becomes cattleless.
Now, whoever, knowing the yoking of the ladles, responds when addressed with "O Adhvaryu!", is not cut off from the deities, becomes possessor of cattle.
Heaven is the juhū,
the atmosphere is the upabhṛt,
the earth is the dhruvā,
the quarters are the butter-holder,
yonder sun is the sruva.
When he says: "Make [them] hear!", therewith he yokes the juhū;
with "Let there be hearing!" the upabhṛt;
with "Sacrifice!" the dhruvā;
with "We who sacrifice..." the butter-holder;
with the vaṣaṭ-call the sruva.
From the juhū he bastes the dhruvā; from the dhruvā, the juhū;
for yonder [heaven] gives to this [earth], this to yonder.
Continuous, uninterrupted food is given to him in this world and in yonder world;
his fame reaches the people before he arrives, who knows thus.

4.1.12.m:
Prajāpati was both ghee and honey.
From where the honey was, thence he created creatures;
therefore from honey there is procreation.
Therefore they do not perform the ritual with honey,
for that is exhausted.
They perform the ritual with ghee;
unexhausted indeed is this ghee belonging to Prajāpati;
unexhausted among the gods is Prajāpati.
He puts it on the Gārhapatya fire.
The wife looks at it;
this indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
he causes her to take hold of the sacrifice.
Moreover, what the wife does in the sacrifice, a pairing is thereby made,
for propagation.
Unfit for sacrifice indeed, not belonging to sacrifice is that which the wife looks at;
he puts it on the Āhavanīya fire;
he purifies it indeed;
he makes it fit for sacrifice, belonging to sacrifice.
He sets it down on the track of the wooden sword,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
"Let god Savitṛ purify you upwards," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies it upwards.
"You are the Vaiśvānara oblation," [he says];
twelve are these utterances;
twelve months are the year;
by means of the year the sacrificer strips away the brilliance, the faculty, the virility of his rival.
On both sides it belongs to Vaiśvānara;
for on both sides it is belonging to Vaiśvānara;
devoid of brilliance, devoid of virility does his rival become;
moreover, he becomes even more sluggish.
The gods and the Asuras were in conflict;
Indra saw this opening;
thereby he overcame the Asuras.
He overcomes his rival by the sacrifice who, knowing thus, sacrifices.
"With ghee they besprinkle the other oblations; with what the ghee?" so speak the theological disputants.
With truth indeed the ghee is besprinkled.
When he says:
"With truth I besprinkle you," [he says];
sight indeed is truth;
the sacrificer gives up his sight.
Closing his eyes he speaks this yajus;
he does not give up his sight.
The ghee is the sacrifice;
the sacrifice is Viṣṇu.
"Through this Viṣṇu strode, thrice he set down his foot; / gathered [is the world] in its dust," [he says].
When, having put it on the cooking fire, he then puts it on the Gārhapatya, then on the Āhavanīya,
this indeed is that: thrice Viṣṇu strides.
"Burnt up is the Rakṣas," [he says],
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
He wipes the ladles;
he purifies them indeed;
he makes them fit for sacrifice, belonging to sacrifice.
"Unsharpened, destroying rivals," [he says];
for these are unsharpened, destroying rivals.
He wipes the sruva;
the male of them he thus sharpens first;
then the juhū,
then the upabhṛt,
then the dhruvā.
Yonder sun is the sruva,
heaven is the juhū,
the atmosphere is the upabhṛt,
the earth is the dhruvā.
These worlds he thus wipes in regular order;
to these worlds he entrusts himself,
for non-injury.
He wipes the juhū first;
the self indeed is the juhū;
he approaches his own self;
then the upabhṛt;
offspring indeed is the upabhṛt;
he approaches offspring,
for offspring is beyond the self as it were;
then the dhruvā;
cattle indeed are the dhruvā;
he approaches cattle,
for cattle are beyond offspring as it were.
If he were to wipe them upward-turned, the seed would be unstable;
if downward-turned, the seed would be unstable;
he wipes them transversely;
transversely indeed is seed emitted;
stable does the seed become.
If he were to wipe the bowl and the handle from the tip, he would bring strife to the Rakṣases and the non-existences;
he wipes the bowl from the tip, the handle from behind;
he does not bring strife to the Rakṣases and the non-existences.
From within he wipes the juhū forwards, from outside backwards;
from outside the upabhṛt forwards, from within backwards;
having brought [the wipe] all around, [he wipes] the dhruvā;
he arranges his vital breaths in regular order.
The juhū has the sacrificer as deity; the upabhṛt has the rival as deity;
taking four times in the juhū, he takes more ghee;
taking eight times in the upabhṛt, [he takes] less;
he makes the rival an underling.

4.1.13.m:
He should set down many [waters].
As many sprinkling waters as he sets down, so many waters are his in yonder world;
therefore many are to be set down.
The sacrificial essence departed from Agni;
becoming a black antelope, it entered the trees.
When he says:
"You are black, dwelling in the lair; become ghee for Agni!",
he thereby secures that very sacrificial essence.
"You are the vedi; I sprinkle you, pleasing to the barhis," [he says];
the barhis is offspring,
the vedi is the earth;
he thereby makes offspring suitable on this [earth].
When, having sprinkled from above, he sprinkles from below,
therefore from rain from above, plants are born below.
"Hold the earth with vigor!" [he says];
he placed vigor in this [earth];
therefore creatures subsist on this [earth].
"You are the crest of Viṣṇu," [he says, and] takes the prastara from the front;
thereby he makes him chief, headed.
He should take as much as he can grasp with his hand;
the sacrificer becomes free from hunger.
He should not wipe upward;
if he were to wipe away whatever is his acquired [wealth] or to be acquired; he should not wipe downward;
what would come to him, that he would thrust away.
He should not shake it out;
he would shake out offspring and cattle.
What might stand upright, that he should gather up.
Just as one heaps up [food] for one who has eaten, even so he spreads the grass-cord on the south;
therefore the undergarment-tuck is on the south.
"Spread wide, soft as wool!" [he says];
he widens it indeed.
"A good seat for the gods!" [he says];
for the gods he makes it.
Holding the prastara, he places the enclosing sticks around;
the sacrificer thereby surrounds himself,
for non-harming.
He does not place [one] in front;
rising indeed, yonder sun smote away the rakṣases;
rising indeed, yonder sun smites away the rakṣases for him.
He places two upright kindling sticks;
thereby he smites away rakṣases from above him.
He places two,
for pairing.
"You two are the dividers," [he says];
he makes them two dividers indeed.
"In the lap of Aditi I seat you," [he says];
Aditi is this [earth];
upon this very [earth] he seats it.
"You are the seat of the Vasus, Rudras, and Ādityas," [he says];
these deities indeed conquered this [seat] in the beginning;
for them he makes this seat.
"You are heaven by birth, juhū by name, dear to the gods; sit in the firm seat with your dear name," [he says];
this indeed is their dear name, that it is ghee-laden;
that very name he secures.
"Protect them, O Viṣṇu;
protect the sacrifice;
protect the lord of the sacrifice;
protect me, the leader of the sacrifice!" [he says];
just as one says: "This is your share, protect it!", so indeed he says to Viṣṇu: "This is your ghee, protect it!"
The vedi departed from the gods;
the gods found it with the veda;
that is the veda-nature of the veda.
The vedi is female,
the veda is male;
when he cleanses the vedi with the veda,
[it is] for pairing.
Spreading continuously from the Āhavanīya fire he goes,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He sacrifices continuously on it in the next fortnight.
The poker is the attendant of the trees;
prosperous become the settlers of him who knows thus.
He throws it down from the east towards the west;
therefore settlers are settled toward the west.
He throws it down from the thicker end;
his settlers do not speak insolently to him.
Indeed, it is performed in the sacrifice for everyone.
The poker is a serpent,
the rubbish-heap is an anthill;
when he throws down the poker on the rubbish-heap,
therefore the anthill is the home of serpents.

4.1.14.m:
"You are being," [he says];
he prays for welfare.
"Spread wide!" [he says];
he widens it indeed.
"Standing from here, Indra performed heroic deed, taking hold together with the deities," [he says];
for standing from here Indra performed heroic deed, taking hold together with the deities.
"Come, O juhū!
Agni calls you;
let us two sacrifice to the gods for divine worship.
Come, O upabhṛt!
God Savitṛ calls you;
let us two sacrifice to the gods for divine worship," [he says].
These two, indeed, are not easy to take by one who does not know;
for he steps upon the bonds of Varuṇa.
He takes them up with these deities,
so as not to step upon the bonds of Varuṇa.
"You are the step of Viṣṇu," [saying which] he strides across,
so as not to step down upon the sacrifice.
"Upward is the rite, touching heaven, uncrooked is the sacrifice of the sacrificer," [saying which] he pours the libation straight and upward,
so that the sacrifice does not stumble.
"Accompanied by Indra, rich in property," [he says];
he makes the sacrifice possess Indra;
moreover, by that he secures sensory power.
"May I stand in your shadow, rich in Vasus," [he says];
Agni indeed is rich in Vasus;
he stands in the shadow of Agni.
He should pour the libation straight and upward;
the libation is breath;
the sacrificer attains full life.
Against one whom he hates, having poured his libation, he should cut it short;
he cuts off his breath;
he dies suddenly.
The head of the sacrifice, indeed, is the libation;
the body is ghee.
If, having poured the libation, he were to anoint it with the upabhṛt, he would cut off the head of the sacrifice.
He anoints it with the butter-holder [saying]: "Anointed with Agni is the oblation, anointed with the oblation is the ghee."
He set the head back upon the body of the sacrifice;
moreover, he sets mind in the sacrifice.
Creatures possessing udders live upon the udder;
"Upon what do those without udders [live]?"—the theological disputants say.
"Licking with water-drops, may the branches extend," [he says];
upon this very thing do they live.
"Becoming a dappled barren cow, go to the Maruts," [he says];
the Maruts indeed rule over rain;
they bring down rain for him.
"Thence aid us with rain," [he says];
he wins rain from the Maruts.
"You are the eye-protector, O Agni;
protect my eye," [he says];
he protects his eye indeed.
"You are the ear-protector, O Agni;
protect my ear," [he says];
he protects his ear indeed.
"You are the body-protector, O Agni;
protect my body," [he says];
he protects his body indeed.
"Whatever of my body is deficient, O Agni, fill that up for me," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Which enclosing-stick you put around yourself, O divine Agni, being pursued by the Paṇis,
that one I bring to you according to your pleasure, lest this one depart from you," [he says];
by a yajus indeed they are yoked, by a yajus they are released.
"You who partake of the overflow are these great ones," [saying which], having thrown in the enclosing-sticks, he offers upon [them];
these are Agnis indeed; he pleases them;
he appeases them with their share.
The theological disputants say:
"That one indeed would be an Adhvaryu
who, from where he yokes the sacrifice, there establishes it."
"O gods finding the way, having found the way, go on the way!
O Lord of Mind, place this sacrifice well in heaven, among the gods, in the wind, svāhā!" [he says];
from here indeed the Adhvaryu yokes the sacrifice;
there itself he establishes it.

4.3.7.m:
Now this triple combination.
As for the former triple combination,
by it he supports these worlds upright from here for him.
As for the latter triple combination,
by it he supports these worlds downward from there for him.
That surely is for holding apart these worlds.
As for the former triple combination, that is the generation of heroes.
As for the latter, that is the generation of cattle.
As for the former triple combination,
heroes indeed he generates for him by it.
As for the latter,
cattle by it.
As for the former triple combination,
seed indeed he places by it.
As for the latter,
he brings forth by it.
With the former triple combination one desirous of sacrifice should sacrifice.
Agni indeed is all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he has taken hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Belonging to Indra indeed is the sacrifice;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
that is the undertaking of the sacrifice.
Now, since there is one for Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he is established in the sacrifice.
With the latter triple combination one desirous of cattle should sacrifice.
Soma indeed is the placer of seed;
Pūṣan is the progenitor of cattle.
Soma indeed places seed for him;
Pūṣan brings forth cattle.
By virile power indeed cattle are brought forth;
Pūṣan brings [them] forth;
by virile power alone Pūṣan brings forth cattle for him.
Now, since there is one for Pūṣan,
cattle indeed are Pūṣan;
he is established in cattle.
Now this [offering] to Vaiśvānara and Varuṇa.
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year;
his own indeed are these seasons;
of them he is the consecrated king.
Varuṇa indeed is the consecrated among the gods.
These two indeed are the stirrers among the gods;
these two are the lords of consecration;
these two rule the consecration;
they two consecrate him.
As many as are his own, they indeed follow him;
the gods follow Varuṇa;
as many as are his own, them he makes compliant to him, not disputing.
The pap is this big;
so big indeed is the body;
as big as his body is, having released it from Varuṇa,
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year,
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
Therefore the teeth of one consecrated by the Rājasūya fall out from old age;
for he establishes him in the year,
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
One who is chronically sick, him he should make sacrifice with this.
By Varuṇa, by evil, indeed he is seized;
therefore he is chronically sick.
Since there is [an oblation] to Varuṇa,
from Varuṇa indeed he frees him by that.
The pap is this big;
so big indeed is the body;
as big as his body is, having released it from Varuṇa,
Agni Vaiśvānara indeed is the year,
he establishes him in the year;
he makes him possess the lifespan of a year.
